      "heading": "Refund Reasons: What Each Store Accepts and Rejects",
      "headingZh": "退款原因：每个商店接受和拒绝什么",
      "text": "Both stores accept refund requests for similar reasons, but the way they process them differs significantly. On the App Store, refund requests are submitted through reportaproblem.apple.com, and you select from a predetermined list of reasons: 'I didn't mean to purchase this,' 'A child made a purchase without permission,' 'The app doesn't work as expected,' 'The app was not downloaded or cannot be found,' 'The purchase was made without my authorization,' and 'I didn't receive the item or it doesn't work.' Each reason maps to a different review path. 'Child made a purchase' requests are reviewed more leniently than 'app doesn't work' requests. 'Didn't mean to purchase' is the most commonly rejected reason. On Google Play, the process is similar but with fewer categories. You visit play.google.com, go to your order history, and select 'Request a refund.' The reasons are: 'I purchased this by accident,' 'I no longer want this purchase,' 'The purchase was made by someone else,' 'The purchase is defective or doesn't work as described,' and 'I didn't receive the item.' Google Play's refund system is more automated — many requests are processed by algorithm rather than human review. This means the outcome is more predictable: if your reason matches a category with a high automatic approval rate, you will likely get a refund. If it does not, you will likely be denied. Apple's system involves more human review, which means the outcome is less predictable but potentially more flexible if you can provide compelling evidence. The practical takeaway: on Google Play, choose a reason that matches the automated approval criteria. On the App Store, choose a reason that matches your actual situation and provide as much evidence as possible.",
