      "heading": "Account Consequences: What Happens When a Refund Goes Wrong",
      "headingZh": "账户后果：退款出错时会发生什么",
      "text": "This is the most important section in this guide, because it describes consequences that are not explained anywhere in either store's refund interface. On the App Store, Apple treats refunds as a customer service feature, not a right. If you request too many refunds, or if Apple determines that a refund request was fraudulent, your Apple ID can be locked from making further purchases. The lock is not a warning — it is immediate and blocks all purchases, including free app downloads, updates, and in-app purchases. The lock can only be removed by paying the outstanding balance or by escalating through Apple Support. Multiple refunds in a short period, especially for the same reason, increase the risk of a lock. On Google Play, the consequences are different. Google does not lock your account for excessive refunds in the same way Apple does. Instead, Google may restrict your ability to request refunds in the future. If you request too many refunds, the refund button may simply disappear from your order history. Google may also flag your account as high-risk, which can affect your ability to use Google Pay or make purchases through other Google services. The more important difference is what happens with chargebacks. On the App Store, a chargeback almost always results in an Apple ID lock. On Google Play, a chargeback may result in your Google Payments account being suspended, which affects not just Play Store purchases but also Google One, YouTube Premium, and other Google services. The practical takeaway: if you use both platforms, treat Apple refunds as a limited resource and Google Play refunds as a more flexible but still finite resource. Never file a chargeback on either platform unless you are prepared to lose access to that account entirely.",
