      "heading": "Subscription Refunds: The Most Common Pain Point on Both Platforms",
      "headingZh": "订阅退款：两个平台上最常见的痛点",
      "text": "Subscriptions are the most common source of refund requests on both platforms, and the policies are notably different. On the App Store, Apple has a specific subscription refund path. When you request a refund for a subscription, Apple evaluates whether you used the subscription after the renewal date. If you did not use the service after the renewal, your refund is more likely to be approved. If you used the service, even briefly, the refund is less likely. Apple also considers whether you canceled the subscription before the renewal date. If you canceled and were still charged, the refund is nearly automatic. If you did not cancel, the refund depends on whether you can demonstrate that you were unaware of the renewal or that the renewal terms were unclear. On Google Play, subscription refunds are more restrictive. Google's policy states that you can request a refund within 48 hours of the charge. After 48 hours, subscription refunds are generally not available, though there are exceptions for defective services or fraudulent charges. Google Play also offers a subscription pause feature, which allows you to pause a subscription for up to three months without canceling — this is a feature the App Store does not have. The practical difference: if you are charged for a subscription renewal on the App Store, you have a reasonable chance of getting a refund if you act quickly and can show you did not use the service. On Google Play, your window is much shorter — 48 hours — and after that, refunds are rare. If you use subscriptions across both platforms, the safest approach is to cancel subscriptions immediately after starting them (the subscription remains active until the end of the period) to avoid unexpected renewals.",
