      "text": "One of the most common and least understood situations is cross-platform purchasing. You buy a subscription on an iPhone but later switch to Android. You buy an app on Google Play but want to use it on an iPad. You make an in-app purchase on one platform and expect it to carry over to the other. The rule is simple: purchases are tied to the store where they were made, not to the app or service. A subscription purchased through the App Store is managed through the App Store, even if you access the same service on Android. An in-app purchase made on Google Play will not transfer to the iOS version of the same app. A game that you bought on Google Play is not available on your iPhone unless the developer offers a cross-platform account system. This has two important implications. First, if you are planning to switch ecosystems, cancel all subscriptions on the old platform and re-subscribe on the new platform. Do not assume that subscriptions will transfer — they will not. Second, if you want a refund for a purchase made on the old platform, you must request it through the old platform's refund system, even if you no longer have access to the device. For App Store purchases, you can use reportaproblem.apple.com from any browser. For Google Play purchases, you can use play.google.com from any browser. The refund process does not require the original device. The practical recommendation: before switching ecosystems, take a full inventory of your purchases and subscriptions on the old platform, cancel everything that can be canceled, and request refunds for any recent purchases you will not use on the new platform. Do this before you sell or wipe the old device, because some refund processes require access to purchase receipts that are stored on the device.",
